{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1992 United States Senate election in Ohio was held on November 3, 1992.","title":"United States Senate election in Ohio, 1992"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Incumbent Democratic U.S. Senator John Glenn won re-election to a fourth term, coinciding with Bill Clinton's narrow win during the presidential election.","title":"United States Senate election in Ohio, 1992"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Glenn's voting percentage of 51% represented the worst performance of his four runs for the Senate, likely due to the presence of third-party candidate Martha Grevatt of the far-left Workers World Party.","title":"United States Senate election in Ohio, 1992"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" As of 2016, this is the last time the Democrats have won the Class 3 Senate Seat from Ohio.","title":"United States Senate election in Ohio, 1992"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 2002 United States Senate election in Alaska was held on November 5, 2002.","title":"United States Senate election in Alaska, 2002"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Incumbent Republican United States Senator Ted Stevens ran for and won a sixth term in the United States Senate.","title":"United States Senate election in Alaska, 2002"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He faced perennial candidate Frank Vondersaar, the Democratic nominee, journalist Jim Sykes, the Green Party nominee, and several other independent candidates in his bid for re-election.","title":"United States Senate election in Alaska, 2002"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Ultimately, Stevens crushed his opponents to win what would be his last term in the Senate, allowing him to win with the largest margin of victory for any Senate election in Alaska, as well as the highest percentage of the vote in any of his elections.","title":"United States Senate election in Alaska, 2002"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Dean Heller
