{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Gray is an unincorporated community in Knox County, Kentucky, United States.","title":"Gray, Kentucky"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The community is located along U.S. Route 25E 4.9 mi west of Corbin.","title":"Gray, Kentucky"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Gray has a post office with ZIP code 40734, which opened on January 25, 1888.","title":"Gray, Kentucky"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Saint Camillus Academy was a Catholic school located in Corbin, Kentucky, established in 1908 by the Sisters of Divine Providence with the help of the Diocese of Covington.","title":"Saint Camillus Academy"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The school originally served students in grades 1-12 and included boarding facilities for girls, but in 2012 was closed and in 2013 sold its building to Corbin Schools System.","title":"Saint Camillus Academy"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" For the 2013-14 and the first semester of the 2014-15 school year it was home to the Corbin Educational Center.","title":"Saint Camillus Academy"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The Corbin Educational Center, or CEC, is a Day Treatment school under the Corbin Independent School District.","title":"Saint Camillus Academy"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" It provides day treatment services for students from Corbin, Williamsburg, and Whitley County.","title":"Saint Camillus Academy"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Over Christmas break of the 2014-15 school year CEC returned to the former Corbin City Utilities building.","title":"Saint Camillus Academy"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The former Saint Camillus site will now be home to the new Corbin Middle School.","title":"Saint Camillus Academy"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Demolition of the current building is set to begin in early 2016 with the construction of the new CMS to follow soon after.","title":"Saint Camillus Academy"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Corbin is a ghost town in British Columbia.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is located in a secluded valley in the Rocky Mountains in the East Kootenay Country of southeastern British Columbia.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was founded in 1908 by Daniel Chase Corbin, president of Nelson and Fort Sheppard Railway.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Between 1908 and 1935 Corbin supported the miners of a huge strip mining operation.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The operation was called "Big Showing."","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Corbin once boasted a population of 600.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The town had its own railway, company store, and a hotel called the Flathead.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" In the spring of 1935 labour troubles began between Corbin Collieries and a local union.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The trouble turned into a bloody riot.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" During the riot special policemen, company officials and miners were injured.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Later the Corbin Collieries closed down their operation.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" Many attempts were made to revive the town and work the "Big Showing."","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":12,"text":" These ventures were not successful.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":13,"text":" In 1951 the town was abandoned.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":14,"text":" In 1973 remnants of the town could still be seen.","title":"Corbin, British Columbia"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Forcht Group of Kentucky "(pronounced "fork")" is a group of companies principally owned by Terry E. Forcht, with corporate headquarters in Lexington, Kentucky and Corbin, Kentucky.","title":"Forcht Group of Ken